{
  "name": "priority-queue",
  "type": "module",
  "private": true,
  "scripts": {
    "test": "node --test test/*.test.js"
  }
}
{
  "id": "code:priority-queue",
  "tier": 3,
  "domain": "data-structures-algorithms",
  "title": "Indexed binary heap with decrease-key, and a shortest-path client",
  "prompt": "Finish `src/heap.js` and `src/dijkstra.js`. `IndexedHeap` is a binary min-heap that also keeps an index from key to position, so `push`, `pop`, `remove`, `decreaseKey`, and `setPriority` each run in O(log n) while `has`, `peek`, and `priorityOf` are O(1). The starting point scans a plain array and leaves the keyed operations unimplemented; replace it with the real structure.\n\nEvery entry has a string key that is unique within the heap and a finite numeric priority; smaller priorities leave first, and negative and fractional priorities are ordinary. Entries with equal priority leave in the order they were pushed. Each entry is stamped with an insertion number when it is pushed, and that number never changes afterwards, so an entry whose priority is raised and then lowered back still leaves before entries pushed after it. `push(key, priority)` adds an entry; `peek()` returns `{ key, priority }` for the next entry without removing it, and `pop()` removes and returns it; both return `undefined` on an empty heap. `has(key)` and `priorityOf(key)` report what is held. `decreaseKey(key, priority)` lowers a priority, accepting an unchanged one. `setPriority(key, priority)` moves a priority in either direction. `remove(key)` drops any held key and returns whether it did. `drain()` empties the heap and returns every entry in pop order. `size` reports how many entries are held.\n\n`src/dijkstra.js` exports `shortestPaths(graph, source)` and `shortestPath(graph, source, target)` over a weighted directed graph — a plain object mapping each node id to an object of successor ids and weights, each weight a finite number greater than zero. Drive the search with the heap: when a shorter route to a node that is already queued turns up, lower that node's priority in place rather than queueing the node twice. `shortestPaths` returns `{ dist, prev }` covering every node of the graph, with `Infinity` and `null` for a node the source cannot reach. Where several shortest routes to a node tie, `prev` names the smallest predecessor id among them, so the answer never depends on the order nodes came off the queue. `shortestPath` returns `{ distance, path }` with `path` running from source to target, or `null` when the target is unreachable.\n\nErrors are specific. A heap key that is not a string raises `TypeError` with message `key must be a string`; a priority that is not a finite number raises `TypeError` with message `priority must be a finite number`; pushing a key that is already held raises `Error` with message `duplicate key: \"a\"`; re-prioritising a key that is not held raises `ReferenceError` with message `unknown key: \"a\"`; and a `decreaseKey` that would raise a priority raises `RangeError` with message `priority must not increase for \"a\"`. A graph that is not a non-null, non-array object raises `TypeError` with message `graph must be an object`; a node whose successors are not such an object raises `TypeError` with message `edge map for node \"a\" must be an object`; an edge or endpoint naming an undeclared node raises `ReferenceError` with message `unknown node: \"q\"`; a weight that is not a positive finite number raises `RangeError` with message `weight must be positive: \"a\" -> \"b\"`; and a non-string source raises `TypeError` with message `source must be a string`. Do not add dependencies; `test/` and `package.json` are fixed and the suite must pass with `node --test test/*.test.js`.",
  "heldOut": false,
  "immutable": ["test", "package.json"],
  "checks": [
    { "id": "tests-pass", "outcome": "the test suite passes", "run": "node --test test/*.test.js" },
    { "id": "no-dependencies", "outcome": "no packages were installed", "run": "test ! -e node_modules" }
  ]
}

